+    // The acquire loads and release stores B3 selects for a fenced MemoryValue.
+    // B3LowerToAir picks LoadAcq*/StoreRel* for every target that is not x86 --
+    // its Load case reads `if (isX86()) kind.effects = true; else kind =
+    // LoadAcq32` -- with no isValidForm gate, so this target has no fallback to a
+    // plain load plus a fence. These must exist or Air::Inst::generate reaches
+    // its unreachable default and aborts codegen partway through the procedure.
+    // testb3's testLoadAcq42 is the test that finds it.
+    //
+    // POWER has no ordered load or store instruction, so the ordering is spelled
+    // out. These carry the strength of the ARM64 ldar/stlr pair they stand in
+    // for, which is RCsc -- so the pair is sequentially consistent, not merely
+    // acquire/release -- and the leading `sync` is what buys that: sync is the
+    // only one of these barriers carrying a StoreLoad edge, and lwsync in its
+    // place would drop exactly that edge. Same reasoning as emitAtomicStrongCAS
+    // above; the load sequence is atomicLoad32's, unchanged.
+    //
+    // This is the "leading sync" convention, and joining it is mandatory rather
+    // than stylistic: jsc-ppc64le-atomics-cas.md 3.2 shows leading-sync and
+    // trailing-sync are each self-consistent but must not be mixed in one
+    // program, because an SC store compiled one way and an SC load compiled the
+    // other leave a store-load pair with no full barrier between them. Every
+    // producer in a bun process is leading-sync -- gcc, clang, WTF::Atomic
+    // through them, and atomicLoad32/64 above.
+    //
+    // The two compilers disagree on the trailing half and the port follows
+    // clang: gcc 16 ends a seq_cst RMW with `isync`, clang 22 with `lwsync`
+    // [MEASURED, same doc 3.1]. clang also has a cheaper *pure acquire* load,
+    // `lwz; cmpd 7,rD,rD; bf- 30,+4; isync` -- a control dependency rather than
+    // a barrier -- and `lwsync; stw` for a pure release store [MEASURED
+    // 2026-09-27, clang 22.1.8, read back with llvm-objdump]. Neither is taken
+    // here, and not only because they are weaker than the ldar/stlr pair: an
+    // `isync` acquire is an acquire *only* because of the conditional branch
+    // placed in front of it, and 3.2 declines to export that fragility to Air,
+    // where the shape of the surrounding code is not this file's to guarantee.
